    question: "“What is Terraform State Management?”",
    slug: "devops-55-what-is-terraform-state-management",
    shortDescription: "[REPORTED] Reported interview question/topic from the supplied source.",
    sampleAnswer: "Terraform state maps the resources declared in configuration to\nreal infrastructure and records attributes Terraform needs to plan\nchanges. For teams, state should normally be stored in a secure remote\nbackend with appropriate access control and state locking where\nsupported. If state is lost, do not blindly recreate resources: first\npreserve the configuration and infrastructure, recover state from\nbackend/versioned backups if available, or reconstruct state by\nimporting existing resources. Secrets can also appear in state, so state\nrequires security controls.",
    detailedAnswer: "Terraform state maps the resources declared in configuration to\nreal infrastructure and records attributes Terraform needs to plan\nchanges. For teams, state should normally be stored in a secure remote\nbackend with appropriate access control and state locking where\nsupported. If state is lost, do not blindly recreate resources: first\npreserve the configuration and infrastructure, recover state from\nbackend/versioned backups if available, or reconstruct state by\nimporting existing resources. Secrets can also appear in state, so state\nrequires security controls.\n\nExample: terraform plan reads configuration + current state + provider\ndata; if the state file is lost, recover a backend backup or import\nexisting infrastructure instead of immediately recreating production\nresources.\n\nInterview Tip: Answer the exact reported question first. Then explain\nthe mechanism, production relevance, and one concrete example. For task\nquestions, describe the implementation steps, validation, failure cases,\nand security/operational considerations.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing: Whether you understand the concept beyond\ntool names, can reason about production behavior, and can troubleshoot\nor implement the solution when asked.\n\nKey Points to Remember: Keep definitions precise; distinguish tools from\nconcepts; explain ownership, state, networking, security, reliability,\nand automation where relevant; and never claim a command or architecture\nis universal when it depends on the environment.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions: How does it work internally? Why would you\nchoose it? What happens if it fails? How would you troubleshoot it? How\nwould you secure it? How would you make the setup production-ready?\n\nCommon Mistakes: Giving a memorized one-line definition, listing tools\nwithout explaining usage, ignoring failure recovery, exposing secrets,\nor confusing Docker/containerization, Kubernetes/orchestration, CI/CD,\nand IaC.",
